\name{BaseRContourLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRContourLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Contour Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads a base R \code{contour()} call as the contour it draws.

\code{contour()} had no processor. \code{base_r_adapter} mapped the call to the type
\code{"contour"} and the factory fell through to the generic processor, so the
layer shipped typed \code{"unknown"} -- and the core's trace factory ends its
dispatch with \verb{throw new Error("Invalid trace type: …")}, so the figure
bound interactively and then failed to construct. #214 stopped that by
typing the call \code{"unknown"} at the adapter, which degrades to a static
image; this replaces the picture with the reading (#218).

The curves come from \code{grDevices::contourLines()}, which is the same
computation \code{contour()} does and takes the same defaults, so nothing here
is a guess about what was drawn:

\preformatted{
contour.default(x = seq(0, 1, length.out = nrow(z)),
                y = seq(0, 1, length.out = ncol(z)),
                z, nlevels = 10, levels = pretty(zlim, nlevels),
                zlim = range(z, finite = TRUE))
}

The payload matches \code{ggplot2_contour_layer_processor.R} exactly -- a list
of curves, each a list of \verb{\{x, y, level\}} -- so the two adapters describe
one chart the same way.
}
