\subsection{\code{BaseRContourLayerProcessor$contour_grid()}}{
  The grid and levels the call drew, or NULL when it drew none

Resolved the way \code{contour.default} resolves it, rather than by a rule
of our own. Its arguments are \verb{(x, y, z, ...)}; a named argument
claims its slot and the unnamed ones fill what is left, in order.
Then, and only then:

\preformatted{
  if (missing(z) && !missing(x) && !is.list(x)) { z <- x; x <- NULL }
  }

which is what makes \code{contour(m)} a contour \emph{of} \code{m} rather than a
chart with \code{m} on the x axis.

Reproducing that matters because the wrapper records a partially
named call: measured, \code{contour(c(10, 20, 30), c(100, 200, 300), z)}
arrives as one unnamed argument plus \verb{y =} and \verb{z =}. A rule that
looked for the matrix and took the unnamed arguments \emph{before} it
found \code{z} already named, never looked at the unnamed one, and
announced the caller's grid on the 0-1 default -- every coordinate
wrong, and nothing raised.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRContourLayerProcessor$contour_grid(args)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
